findings.description:
activeGuildId treats a guild plot whose guild no longer exists as unowned. Search shows it vacant (housingSearch.cpp:75-77), the record sends guild -1, and handlePurchase sells it to the next guild.

The purchase rewrites the owner, guild and upkeep fields and the access list, but leaves the plot's balances, its placed and stored items, and its children (the hall with everything in it). The buyer therefore gets:
- the escrow, which its Trustees and Co-Owners can withdraw;
- the hall, a NO TRADE deed (halls 38-41, STT-4);
- the former members' items, through Remove Item.

Before that, nobody can sell the plot or withdraw from it (housingSell.cpp:183-185, housingAccount.cpp:196-198). World keeps charging it and never reclaims guild plots. A guild that disbands next to a partner guild ready to buy can hand over a NO TRADE hall and its contents, a path the transfer check (7637) closes for player plots.

A plot's record and access list now reach only:
- its owner;
- its guild;
- characters it names in a group above 0.

Several permissions are checked by the client itself and need the plot's record and list on the client (no list means deny):
- Add Item 9 and Move Item 10, in the placement rules and the Items window;
- Remove 3, 5 and 8, in the stored-item rules.

So if an owner grants any of these to Everyone, a stranger can no longer use them. Before ZIN-1, every hosted list went to everyone in the zone. ZIN-1's tests covered named groups and Enter, which the server checks, but not this case. Whether Interact Item 11 is also client-gated for world clicks (0x5b0200 / 0x5b75f0) is inferred, not read.

When the seller is a GM, the owner checks are skipped; the client also shows Sell to a GM as if they were the owner (flows/purchase.md section 4). The sale then:
- pays the escrow to the GM;
- puts the placed crate, packed with the owner's houses and items, into the GM's inventory;
- sets the crate property's owner to the GM (:296-299).

The plan's S7 lets only the owner, or a guild rank with Real Estate: Guild Plot - Sell, sell.

missedItems.description:
Live gives a character standing in a yard, or inside one of the plot's houses, the records, access lists and item lists of that plot and everything on it (its houses and crate), whatever the character's relation to it. Peridot's ZIN-1 view gives an unnamed stranger nothing while in the yard; a border-stone click reveals the plot's record only. Inside the house it gets the line's records with no lists and no crate record. ZIN-1's done result, the comment at housing.cpp:243 and serverPlan.md:366 ('standing in it resolves nothing, as on Live') say the opposite of Live's captures. Effects:
- standing-in never resolves for a visitor in a yard;
- an Everyone grant of a client-checked permission is refused by the visitor's own client (A-04's symptom);
- the item display and Inspect fall back to the server (0x58c7).
ZIN-15 (open) is a different question: what a character keeps after losing access.

